{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794734033,"nonce":"Pz2t+amPd5L6rw8NWRJLWZKxdHo=","title":"hxn package cannot build a HashLink .hdll for Intel macOS from an Apple-silicon machine","message":"`hxn package hl --target x86_64-apple-darwin` builds the static library a mobile HashLink program links, not the `.hdll` stock HashLink loads on an Intel Mac, and hxn documents that a host `.hdll` must be built on the system it runs on. GitHub no longer offers Intel macOS runners (a job asking for one waits indefinitely), so the release workflows of hxn libraries cannot produce one, and a Haxe developer on an Intel Mac using HashLink has to install Rust and run `hxn package` there.\n\nhxcpp is covered: the release builds `hxn package cpp --target x86_64-apple-darwin`, and hxcpp links that static library.\n\nA dylib for x86_64-apple-darwin cross-compiles from arm64 macOS with the same SDK (hlwgpu's release does this with plain cargo for xgpu.hdll), so `--target \u003ctriple\u003e` where the triple's OS is the host's could build the `.hdll` as it does for the host, linked against an x86_64 libhl.\n\nAffects hxn-window and hxn-av releases (Intel macOS lists hxcpp only).","files":null}]}